Superficial abrasion from fall on concrete to knee (from propulsion with wheels)
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Weeping raw epidermal defect, Embedded particulate debris, Forward friction fall mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, extensive skin loss and embedded debris following a high-velocity fall, requiring urgent pain control, debridement, and barrier dressing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a high-velocity forward fall from a wheeled device resulting in a large, contaminated abrasive wound. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to thoroughly debride embedded concrete particulate matter, assess for deep structural penetration, and apply specialized occlusive dressings to prevent severe infection and manage intense neuropathic pain prior to daytime clinics opening.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I fell hard off my skateboard onto the concrete; my knee is completely raw, weeping, and burning terribly.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of a forward fall onto a rough, abrasive surface while in motion on a wheeled device 1 hour ago. This resulted in an extensive superficial loss of the epidermis and upper dermis specifically over the patellar region. The wound surface appears raw, weeping, and intensely red with numerous punctate capillary bleeding spots. The patient complains of severe burning and stinging pain that is severely exacerbated by joint movement or exposure to ambient air. Microscopic gravel and concrete dust are visible within the shallow wound bed.
Functional Status: Independent, walking with a stiff-legged gait to avoid bending the knee and stretching the raw skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Engaging in wheeled sports without protective knee pads.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate acute distress related to exposed nerve endings, holding the knee rigidly extended.
Physical Exam: Knee examination reveals a weeping raw epidermal defect measuring 8x8cm. Embedded particulate debris is noted superficially. The edges of the wound are completely irregular and gradually blend into the surrounding normal skin. There is minimal to mild underlying soft tissue swelling restricted strictly to the prepatellar bursa region. A total absence of deep lacerations exposing the patellar tendon or true joint capsule is confirmed. Intact active knee extension, a pain-free straight leg raise, and full weight-bearing capacity are present. Distal neurovascular status is normal.
Environmental Observations: Skateboard and helmet present; patient advised on purchasing knee pads.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial abrasion to knee - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Open joint laceration/traumatic arthrotomy - Ruled out due to: Absence of deep tissue penetration, intact joint capsule, and lack of synovial fluid leakage.

Patellar fracture - Ruled out due to: Pain-free straight leg raise against gravity and ability to comfortably bear weight through the extended leg.

Prepatellar bursitis (traumatic) - Ruled out due to: Primary pathology being superficial epidermal loss, without the presence of a large, fluctuant, localized fluid sac anterior to the patella.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

High-velocity friction abrasions over major joints carry a significant risk for traumatic arthrotomy, occult fractures, and severe environmental contamination. Bedside clinical evaluation successfully confirmed intact joint mechanics and isolated the injury to the superficial epidermis. Urgent after-hours intervention was clinically mandated to perform highly painful, necessary mechanical debridement of concrete particulate matter under local anesthetic gel, and to apply immediate occlusive silicone dressings. Delaying this until daytime hours would risk permanent traumatic tattooing of the skin and severe secondary bacterial infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied topical anesthetic gel. Performed aggressive high-volume syringe irrigation with sterile saline and gentle mechanical scrubbing to remove all microscopic gravel. Applied a non-adherent silicone foam dressing to seal the wound bed. Tetanus status updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Leave the specialized silicone dressing completely intact for 3 to 5 days without lifting the edges to look at it. Maintaining this dark, moist, and undisturbed environment allows the epithelial cells to rapidly migrate across the wound bed, halving the healing time and dramatically reducing painful scarring.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Silicone foam dressings, protective joint netting.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for scheduled dressing change and wound inspection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the knee suddenly becomes massively swollen, hot to the touch, if thick foul-smelling yellow pus drains from under the dressing, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Abrasions over highly mobile joints are prone to delayed healing and secondary infection due to constant mechanical stretching.
Clinical Course: The severe stinging pain will vanish entirely within 24 hours under the occlusive dressing. The dressing will absorb large amounts of yellow fluid; this is normal wound exudate, not infection. Full healing takes 10-14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if redness starts spreading rapidly down your shin or up your thigh, or if you completely lose the ability to straighten your leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specialized dressing acts as an artificial scab. It stops the air from hitting the raw nerve endings (which stops the pain) and traps the body's natural healing fluids right against the wound.
Actionable Steps: Do not let the dressing soak in the shower. Do not rip the dressing off, even if it looks puffy with fluid. Wear knee pads for future wheeled activities.

